From 378e2bf29b5ab89dc6c279cc7cead38a515936c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Ben Scholzen (DASPRiD)" <mail@dasprids.de>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 18:05:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Type-ahead find

Typing in a folder selects the first file whose name starts with what was
typed, instead of starting a recursive search. A small entry at the bottom
left of the view shows the query. Switchable through the type-ahead-find
GSetting, with a switch in Preferences.

Keys reach the entry through gtk_editable_set_input_interceptor(), new in
GTK 4.24, which runs the entry's own input method on the view itself, so
dead keys and input methods work although the entry never takes the focus.
Leaving the focus where it is keeps the view's own navigation and selection
keys working while a query is up.

Backspace is the one key the view cannot be left to resolve, since the
window resolves its managed shortcuts, "go to parent folder" among them, in
the bubble phase. A capture-phase controller on the view takes it while the
entry is visible and edits the query instead.

The entry goes away a second after the last key, and on anything which is
not more typing: a click in the view, a key which moves the cursor, Escape,
the focus leaving the view, or a change of folder or view mode. A query
which outlives the attention it was typed with would otherwise keep
Backspace and extend into whatever is typed next.

Matching walks the view's own model, which is already sorted and filtered
and has expanded folders flattened in place, so it follows the order on
screen, never lands on a hidden file, and finds items inside expanded
folders in list view. Names and query are both casefolded and normalized.
